## Components

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| Domain name | `foobar.com`, with a `www` record pointing to the server IP `8.8.8.8` |
| Server | 1 server (`8.8.8.8`) hosting all the components below |
| Web server | Nginx |
| Application server | Runs the application code |
| Application files | The code base of the website |
| Database | MySQL |

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

1. The user types `www.foobar.com` in the browser.
2. The browser asks the DNS to translate the name into an IP address. The DNS
   answers with the `www` record of `foobar.com`: `8.8.8.8`.
3. The browser sends an HTTP (or HTTPS) request over TCP/IP to `8.8.8.8`.
4. The request reaches the server, where **Nginx** receives it.
5. For static files (images, CSS, JS), Nginx answers directly. For dynamic
   content, it forwards the request to the **application server**.
6. The application server runs the code of the **application files** and, when
   it needs data, queries the **MySQL** database.
7. The database returns the data, and the application server builds the page
   and sends it back to Nginx.
8. Nginx sends the HTTP response to the user's browser, which displays the
   website.

## Specifics

### What is a server?
A server is a computer (physical or virtual) that provides resources, data or
services to other computers, called clients, over a network. Here, a single
server hosts everything needed to run the website.

### What is the role of the domain name?
The domain name (`foobar.com`) is the human-readable address of the website.
It is easier to remember than an IP address, and the DNS translates it into the
IP address of the server (`8.8.8.8`).

### What type of DNS record is `www` in `www.foobar.com`?
`www` is an **A record** (Address record): it maps a hostname directly to an
IPv4 address, here `8.8.8.8`.

### What is the role of the web server?
The web server (Nginx) receives the HTTP/HTTPS requests from users. It serves
static content directly and forwards dynamic requests to the application
server, then returns the response to the user.

### What is the role of the application server?
The application server executes the application code (the business logic). It
handles dynamic requests, communicates with the database, and generates the
dynamic content that is sent back to the user.

### What is the role of the database?
The database (MySQL) stores, organizes and retrieves the persistent data of the
website (users, articles, orders...). The application server reads and writes
this data with SQL queries.

### How does the server communicate with the computer of the user?
Over the Internet, using the **TCP/IP** protocol suite, with **HTTP** (or
**HTTPS**, which is HTTP secured with TLS) as the application protocol.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SPOF (Single Point Of Failure)
There is only one server. If it fails (hardware problem, crash, power or
network outage), or if one component on it fails (Nginx, application server or
MySQL), the whole website goes down.

### Downtime when maintenance is needed
Maintenance, such as deploying new code (which requires restarting the web
server) or updating the system, makes the website unavailable, because no other
server can take over in the meantime.

### Cannot scale if there is too much incoming traffic
All the components share the CPU, RAM, disk and network of one machine. If the
traffic grows too much, the server becomes overloaded and slow, and the load
cannot be spread across several servers. The only option is to upgrade the
machine (vertical scaling), which has limits.
